The [CODE marker tiddler](#vocab/markdown/CODE) uses `open-variable: yes` so the backtick delimiter can be any length the author chooses. The opening run sets `N`; the FIRST closing run of EXACTLY `N` backticks (not flanked by another backtick on either side) ends the span. Body is captured verbatim — no inline parsing inside. Single-leading/trailing-space stripping kicks in when both flanks have a space AND the body has any non-whitespace.

## Pattern 1 — single backtick

The basic case: one backtick opens, one backtick closes.

Live: `code`

Source:

```
`code`
```

## Pattern 2 — double backticks WITH a literal single backtick inside

This is the point of variable-length runs. The inner single `` ` `` doesn't match the 2-run closing delimiter, so it stays as literal content. With only single-backtick code spans, the inner `` ` `` would have to be escaped or worked around; with a longer delimiter, it just stays as itself.

Live: ``contains ` tick``

Source:

````
``contains ` tick``
````

## Pattern 3 — triple backticks with a literal double backtick inside

Same idea, one level up. The 3-run delimiter only closes on a same-length 3-run; inner `` `` `` runs are content.

Live: ```contains `` two```
Live: `contains `` two`

Source:

````
```contains `` two```
or 
`contains `` two`
````

## Pattern 4 — leading/trailing space stripping

When the captured body has both a leading and trailing space AND any non-whitespace content, ONE leading and ONE trailing space are stripped. That lets you wrap content that starts or ends with `` ` ``:

Live: `` ` not a closing tick ` ``

Source:

````
`` ` not a closing tick ` ``
````

The leading/trailing single spaces are consumed by the strip, so the rendered body starts and ends with a literal backtick.

## Pattern 5 — no matching close: backtick stays literal

If the opening run has no same-length partner within the same paragraph, the backticks fall through to plain text. The parser advances past the run without claiming it as a span. (Same-paragraph scope means an unmatched backtick can't accidentally swallow a later paragraph or a fenced source block.)

Live: a `lonely backtick has no partner

Source:

```
a `lonely backtick has no partner
```

## How the matching works

At parse time the engine:

1. Finds a maximal run of backticks flanked by non-backticks (or string boundaries) — call its length `N`.
2. Walks forward looking for the FIRST same-length run with the same flanking property — but only until the next blank line. Runs of length ≠ N are skipped (they're content).
3. If no such close exists within the current paragraph, falls back to emitting the opening backticks as plain text.
4. If found, captures the body verbatim, applies the single-space strip when applicable, and emits a `<code>` element (or whatever the marker's `element` field declares).

## DSL flexibility

`open-variable: yes` is a marker field, not a hardcoded engine behaviour. Any inline-pair marker can opt into variable-length delimiter runs. A DSL that wants its own variable-length wrappers can ship a marker with `open: ~`, `open: ^`, or any other single character — same matching semantics, different delimiter. See [Marker Fields](#Marker Fields) for the field reference.

The semantic this configuration captures is **"literal verbatim text that markdown shouldn't parse, with a delimiter that can grow to escape itself"**. Any HTML element whose body fits that description is a candidate. The patterns below are **illustrative** — none of them are shipped in this wiki — but each shows a coherent marker tiddler that could be added.

### `<kbd>` — keyboard input

```
title: vocab/markdown/KBD
kind: inline-pair
allow-symbol: no
body-raw: yes
open: ~
close: ~
open-variable: yes
element: kbd
classes: .wltc-md-kbd
```

Usage: `~Ctrl+C~` → `<kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd>`. The variable-length matters when the keystroke description itself contains `~`: `~~press the literal ~ key~~`.

### Inline math (LaTeX content)

```
title: vocab/markdown/MATH
kind: inline-pair
allow-symbol: no
body-raw: yes
open: $
close: $
open-variable: yes
element: span
classes: .wltc-md-math
```

Usage: `$E = mc^2$` → `<span class="wltc-md-math wltc">E = mc^2</span>`. With KaTeX or MathJax wired up client-side, the span gets typeset as a real equation. Variable-length lets authors quote LaTeX that uses `$` inside (`$$\sum_i a_i$$`) without escaping.

### `<samp>` — sample / program output

```
title: vocab/markdown/SAMP
kind: inline-pair
allow-symbol: no
body-raw: yes
open: =
close: =
open-variable: yes
element: samp
classes: .wltc-md-samp
```

Usage: `=Permission denied=` → `<samp>Permission denied</samp>`. Semantically distinct from `<code>` (output rather than source); a docs tiddler can style them differently. The delimiter `=` is unused elsewhere in vocab/markdown, but a DSL author should always check for collisions before picking one.

### When NOT to set `open-variable`

For markers whose body is prose, not literal — emphasis (`*italic*`, `**bold**`), strikethrough (`~~struck~~`), even superscript / subscript — variable-length is overkill and may cause confusing matches. Authors won't typically write `*` or `~` inside emphasized prose, and the body-parsing semantics (inline children vs verbatim) differ anyway. Reserve `open-variable: yes` for the verbatim-body, escaping-needed use cases above.

